  "strategy": "Approach and rationale:\nThe central idea is to directly minimize the maximum overlap between the function h and its shifted versions by formulating a convex optimization problem. Unlike the discrete coordinate descent method, this approach preserves the continuous shift domain by using an equivalent representation based on the periodization and inverse Fourier transform. The key is to bypass the combinatorial complexity and directly optimize the function in the frequency domain, where the overlap constraint becomes separable. \n\nThis addresses the bottleneck of getting stuck in local minima by allowing global searches through the frequency domain. The mathematical equivalence between the constraints in time and frequency domains ensures that a solution in the Fourier space maps uniquely back to the time domain, making it feasible to find global optima. The gradient-based optimization with projected gradient steps guarantees both constraint satisfaction and gradient information for faster convergence. The resolution adaptation transfers feasibility between scales systematically.\n\nMathematical specification:\n1. Representation: Treat h as a function on [0,2] with integral 1 and bounded between 0 and 1.\n2. Objective: Minimize C\u2085 = max\u2096 [\u222b\u2080\u00b2 h(x)(1 - h(x+k)) dx]\n3. Equivalence transformation:\n   - Periodize h to p: h_p( t) = h({t} * 2/2) (where {t} is fractional part)\n   - Let \u00db = F(h) be the Fourier transform of p (periodized)\n   - The constraint \u222b\u2080\u00b2 h(x)(1 - h(x+k)) dx becomes tridiagonal in frequency domain\n4. Optimization variables: \u0124 = F(h), a function with specific frequency domain properties\n5. Adaptation: For grid size n, solution H\u2099 satisfies sum(H\u2099) = n/2 and constrains every element between 0 and 1\n6. Mathematical assurance: The Fourier transform representation maintains strict mathematical equivalence to the original problem\n\nImplementation procedure:\n1. Input: n_points resolution (initially 600 if parent available)\n2. Fourier transform initialization:\n   - Apply periodization and standard Fourier transform\n   - Compute constraint diagonal elements\n   - Formulate feasibility constraints in frequency domain\n3. Projected gradient iteration:\n   - Compute current maximum overlap coefficient\n   - Formulate a convex surrogate problem\n   - Solve with projected gradient method\n   - Domain: \u0124 \u2208 \u211d[0,2] with bounds F(H) matching given n_points\n   - Projection step ensures unit integral and bounds\n4. Phase transition (resolutions change):\n   - Use interpolation preserving moments\n   - Recompute frequency coefficients\n   - Continue optimization with same stopping criteria\n5. Stalling detection: Relies on monitorinegm Redux value iterations\n\nSearch choices and variations:\n1. Primary method: Projection onto frequency domain with gradient descent\n2. Hyperparameters:\n   - Starting grid: 600 (if parent; otherwise 200)\n   - Learning rate: 0.01\n   - Gradient clipping: 0.1\n   - Minimum change threshold: 1e-7\n3. Weights variation: Use increased smoothing for larger grids (\u03b1=0.1)\n\nComputebudget androbustness:\n1. Dominant cost: O(n logn) FFT per projection\n2. Time budget targets:\n   - Initial phase: 300s for n=600\n   - Each doubling: 400s\n3. Fallback: Preserve closest valid candidate found\n4. Early termination: \n   - Objective plateau detection (r = 1e-5)\n   - Null grid validation checks\n\nValidation and return contract:\n1. For final solution verify:\n   - sum(h) == n/2 exactly\n   - All elements in [0,1]\n   - recompute C\u2085 using exact coreelate method",
